    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. that's pretty good. can you make it sound a little more personal though? like maybe something that acknowledges how much work they put in without being too cheesy. i want them to feel like they actually made an impact even if it didn't work out.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To make it sound more personal, you need to focus on the *quality* of their contribution, not just the *time* they spent. You can weave in a specific, genuine compliment about their interview performance. This shows you were actually listening to them, which is much more impactful than a generic thank you.\n\nHere is a revised version that has a warmer, more personal touch.\n\n***\n\n**Subject: Update on the Software Engineer Position at [Company Name]**\n\nDear [Candidate Name],\n\nThank you again for investing so much time and energy into the interview process for the Software Engineer role. We genuinely appreciated the depth of insight you shared with our team; your thoughtful responses during the final rounds were truly impressive. It was a very competitive search, and the decision was incredibly difficult.\n\nWhile we have decided to move forward with another candidate whose background was a slightly tighter fit for our current project scope, please know that your skills and perspective made a real impact on our discussions. We were particularly impressed by [mention a specific skill or project they discussed, e.g., your approach to scaling databases or your insights on front-end architecture].\n\nWe truly hope you find a role where your talents can shine as brightly as they",
